[[Image:Lonicera xylosteum Sturm45.jpg|left|176 px|thumb|''Lonicera xylosteum'']]

'''''Lonicera xylosteum''''', commonly known as '''fly honeysuckle''',<ref name="rhs">{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/10530/Lonicera-xylosteum/Details |title=Fly honeysuckle |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=RHS Gardening |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=31 August 2017}}</ref> '''European fly honeysuckle''', '''dwarf honeysuckle'''<ref>{{cite web |url=https://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile?symbol=LOXY |title=Plants profile for Lonicera xylosteum |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=Plants database |publisher=Natural Resources Conservation Service |access-date=31 August 2017}}</ref> or '''fly woodbine''' is a [[deciduous]] shrub.<ref name="rhs" />

Its fruit [[persistence (botany)|persists]] for an average of 15.4 days, and bears an average of 4.5 seeds per fruit. Fruits average 88.6% water, and their [[dry matter|dry weight]] includes 43.4% [[carbohydrate]]s and 1.2% [[lipid]]s.{{sfn|Ehrlén|Eriksson|1991}}

The glossy red (or occasionally yellow) berries of this shrub are mildly poisonous to humans&nbsp;–&nbsp;children who ingest a large number (c. 30) of berries may experience abdominal pain and vomiting.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cbif.gc.ca/eng/species-bank/canadian-poisonous-plants-information-system/all-plants-scientific-name/lonicera-xylosteum/?id=1370403266925 |title=Lonicera xylosteum |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=Canadian Poisonous Plants Information System |publisher=Canadian Biodiversity Information Facility |access-date=31 August 2017}}</ref>

